      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-80",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{4}+9x}{6{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-80.json",
          "file_sha256": "60c5ed6a766c656a303b73c5804deda1b6ed503e713cfef053486c1ced6c746e"
        }
