    {
      "question": "e-81",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "e0867f8000c144d89e3926661023c9d9e6d016a149b94d2b454488ef7f99e755",
      "new_sha256": "b192f1009c6a6bb11b8d95f3f28e88ac78b882d22da8dc00ad879938a4185a03",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-81",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4-5x-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-2x-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{-2x+0+0}{1+0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-81.json",
