    {
      "question": "e-82",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "a5b19c07023ee156df361f5478c0f143faf653c138c2dd3ae0105d635c124aaf",
      "new_sha256": "d332c78591f1c2f812db6cf95833e5af8beef8c698b275d7bc6ba5f435a07b80",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-82",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061351/example-82.json",
          "file_sha256": "0192f58ca228592b65fe2aefd67c59c3311a0927511ccc127e1aabeced00de0f"
